Let us trace the logic with a small example like nums = [3, 0, 1]. The size of the array n is 3. The expected sum of numbers from 0 to 3 is calculated as 3 * (3 + 1) / 2, which equals 6. The loop adds the elements in the array: 3 + 0 + 1 = 4. Subtracting the array sum from the expected sum gives exactly 6 - 4 = 2. The missing number is indeed 2.

1. VERDICT
Correct: your code correctly uses the arithmetic sum formula to find the exact missing value efficiently in linear time.
2. REVIEW MY CODE

* What I did well: You correctly used Gauss's formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers, which avoids needing extra memory or sorting.
* Is it correct? Yes, it passes all test cases perfectly.
* Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical bugs. The code handles arrays missing the last number, arrays missing 0, and large arrays without issue. The only minor risk is integer overflow if n was extremely large, but LeetCode constraints limit n to 10000, so n*(n+1)/2 fits safely in a standard 32-bit signed integer.
* Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(n) because you iterate through the array exactly once. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space because you only store a few primitive integer variables.
* Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: If you have a sequence from 0 to n, adding all those numbers gives you a known total. If one number is missing, the actual sum of your array will fall short of that known total by exactly the value of the missing number.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Sorting (Brute Force)
Idea: Sort the array first. Then walk through the array checking if the number at index i matches the value i. The first mismatch is the missing number.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        for(int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++){
            if(nums[i] != i){
                return i;
            }
        }
        return nums.size();
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n log n) due to the sorting step. Space complexity is O(1) or O(log n) depending on the sorting algorithm used under the hood.
What makes the next approach better: Sorting takes more than linear time. We can use extra memory to look up numbers instantly in O(n) time without altering the array.

Approach 2: Hash Set
Idea: Put all the numbers from the array into a hash set. Then loop from 0 to n and check which number is missing from the set.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_set>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> numSet(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        for(int i = 0; i <= nums.size(); i++){
            if(numSet.find(i) == numSet.end()){
                return i;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) to build the set and check values. Space complexity is O(n) auxiliary space to store the set.
What makes the next approach better: Hash sets use extra memory. We can do it in O(1) space using math or bitwise operations.

Approach 3: Math Formula (Your Approach)
Idea: Calculate the expected sum of 0 to n, then subtract the actual sum of the array. The difference is the missing number.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int expectedSum = n * (n + 1) / 2;
        int actualSum = 0;
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
            actualSum += nums[i];
        }
        return expectedSum - actualSum;
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) for one loop. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space.
What makes the next approach better: If n is extremely large, multiplying n by n+1 can cause an integer overflow. Bitwise XOR completely avoids overflow risks.

Approach 4: Bit Manipulation (XOR)
Idea: The XOR operator cancels out identical numbers (a XOR a = 0). If we XOR all the index numbers from 0 to n, and also XOR all the values in the array, every number will appear twice and cancel out, leaving only the missing number behind.
C++ code:

```cpp
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int missingNumber(vector<int>& nums) {
        int missing = nums.size();
        for(int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++){
            missing ^= i ^ nums[i];
        }
        return missing;
    }
};

```

Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) for one loop. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space.

4. WHERE I STAND

* Which approach did I use? You used Approach 3, the Math Formula.
* Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? Interviewers expect either the Math approach or the XOR approach. If you provide the Math approach, they will likely ask about the XOR approach as a follow-up.
* Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
Question: What if the array is so large that the sum formula causes an integer overflow?
Answer: You can use the XOR approach. Since XOR operates bit by bit, the running total never exceeds the maximum number in the sequence, making it completely safe from overflow.

